From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 10:56:27 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: widen the FRITZ!Box 7590 to its full 512 MiB

The memory node declared 224 MiB of a 512 MiB board. That ceiling was
never about the part: it was the boundary of the 4Kec bootcore's window.
A dual-kernel image starts that bootcore alongside this kernel, and the
bootcore blob in it declares ram-size = <256>; the bootcore turns that
number into a PM_DDR_GROUP_NONE deny-all region over everything above
it, so every initiator loses the upper half of populated DRAM. The
vendor describes that second CPU and its own memory window in its
bootcore device tree (grx550_bootcore.dtsi).

A single-kernel image removes the premise rather than working around it.
It starts no bootcore, so the protection pass never runs and the deny-all
region never exists. With no fabric denial there is nothing between this
kernel and the rest of the DRAM, so declare all of it.

The consequence is a hard coupling, and the comment above the node says
so at length: this DTB is single-kernel-only. Booting it from a
dual-kernel image reinstates the deny-all across the upper half of the
range it declares, and the failure is a fault or a hang on first touch up
there, not a diagnosable message.

Add the shm rng seed reservation at 0x3dfb0000, which could not be
declared while the node ended at 224 MiB. The vendor states the address
absolutely on both cores. It declares the region without no-map and with
an avm,shm-rng-seed-early compatible, because its driver reads the buffer
from a RESERVEDMEM_OF_DECLARE hook; mainline has no such driver, so this
is a pure address reservation -- compatibles dropped, no-map added,
because nothing here should map or read it.

Keep the cpunet and bootcore reservations. They stop being free at this
width: while the node ended at 0x2e000000 the bootcore window was outside
memblock.memory and its no-map was a supported no-op, whereas now both
fall inside and 32 MiB plus 256 KiB come off usable RAM for real. That is
worth paying. They are inert on the single-kernel boots this DTB is for,
and they are what makes a dual-kernel boot survivable rather than
silently corrupting; they should be deleted with the dual-kernel chain,
not before it.

One bound did not move. The uncached KSEG1 alias of DRAM still ends at
PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M = 0x30000000, so general dma_alloc_coherent()
consumers must stay below it. Cached RAM is fine across the whole node,
since under EVA's SegCtl KSEG0 maps 0x80000000 to 0x20000000 and spans
all 512 MiB.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
index 1c341e2bd329..9ff2a40bb044 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
@@ -27,29 +27,42 @@ avm-hw-revision {
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * 224 MiB of the board's 512 MiB, at PHYS_OFFSET 0x20000000.
+	 * The board's full 512 MiB, at PHYS_OFFSET 0x20000000.
 	 *
-	 * The ceiling is not a guess and it is not the part's size. Two
-	 * separate things sit above it:
+	 * ## THIS DTB IS SINGLE-KERNEL-ONLY.                      ##
+	 * ## Booting it from a dual-kernel container corrupts or  ##
+	 * ## hangs, and it does so silently at first.             ##
 	 *
-	 * The 4Kec bootcore. It runs concurrently with this kernel on every
-	 * dual-kernel boot, out of the 32 MiB window at 0x2e000000, with its
-	 * cpunet mailbox pool immediately below. Ending the memory node
-	 * exactly at 0x2e000000 keeps Linux and the bootcore out of each
-	 * other's DRAM by construction. Protecting: [0x30000000, 0x9fffffff]"
-	 * on this silicon. Anything this kernel places above 0x30000000 would
-	 * be denied by the fabric, not merely shared.
+	 * The coupling is exact and it is not a style preference. The
+	 * bootcore's protect_unattached() takes the ram-size = <256> its own
+	 * device tree declares and programs a PM_DDR_GROUP_NONE deny-all
+	 * fabric region over its 32 MiB window at [0x2e000000, 0x2fffffff]
+	 * and over everything above that size, [0x30000000, 0x9fffffff].
 	 *
-	 * Widening past this needs the bootcore question settled first (a
-	 * bootcore built for 512 MiB, or a single-kernel image that starts no
-	 * bootcore at all) and the KSEG1 aperture bound respected -- see the
-	 * warning on the reservations below. It also cannot move the CBM pools
-	 * that seale_avm.dtsi reserves at 0x23800000..0x25200000, only extend
-	 * past them.
+	 * The upper half of this board's populated DRAM -- precisely the half
+	 * this memory node now declares -- is then denied to every initiator.
+	 * The failure is a fault or hang on first touch of anything above
+	 * 0x30000000, and it is not the KSEG1-aperture class and not helped
+	 * by staying under that bound.
+	 *
+	 * A single-kernel image starts no bootcore, so the protection pass
+	 * never runs and this node is safe.
+	 *
+	 * Two bounds that did NOT move and still apply at 512 MiB:
+	 *
+	 * The uncached KSEG1 alias of DRAM ends at PHYS_OFFSET + 0x12310000 =
+	 * 0x32310000 (~291 MiB), measured. Cached RAM is fine across the
+	 * whole node -- under EVA's SegCtl, KSEG0 maps 0x80000000 to
+	 * 0x20000000 and covers all 512 MiB -- but general
+	 * dma_alloc_coherent() consumers must stay below 0x32310000. hdma is
+	 * already re-bound to that aperture; a new uncached consumer is what
+	 * would break here first, not the node width. The CBM pools at
+	 * 0x23800000..0x25200000 that seale_avm.dtsi reserves cannot move,
+	 * only be extended past.
 	 */
 	memory@20000000 {
 		device_type = "memory";
-		reg = <0x20000000 0x0e000000>;
+		reg = <0x20000000 0x20000000>;
 	};
 
 	aliases {
@@ -104,6 +117,15 @@ bootcore@2e000000 {
 			reg = <0x2e000000 0x2000000>;
 			no-map;
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Fixed-address seed buffer shared with the second CPU;
+		 * reserved no-map so nothing here maps or reads it.
+		 */
+		shm-rng-seed@3dfb0000 {
+			reg = <0x3dfb0000 0x1000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
 	};
 };
 
